This is a Flow viewport toolkit example. 
The Flow viewport toolkit has been developed to :
- create hydra primitives directly without the need to create OpenUSD data or Maya geometry 
- add custom hydra filtering scene indices to MayaHydra to modify the hydra primitives in the viewport

This project is a Maya node that creates a Hydra primitive and applies to it the custom GLSL shader for Hydra Storm that is created and registered in OpenUSD/hydra by the project 
https://github.com/Autodesk/maya-hydra/tree/dev/lib/flowViewport/usdPlugins/shadersDiscoveryPlugin

It also shows how to use the Flow viewport MaterialCreationInterface to replace Maya GLSLShader materials
by a MayaHydra GLSL shader material :
- [mhCustomVertexBufferGenerator.cpp](./mhCustomVertexBufferGenerator.cpp) registers a VP2 vertex buffer generator filling the *"myCustomStreamB"* custom stream requested by the *myCustomShader.ogsfx* shader.
- [mhCustomMaterialCallbackCmd.cpp](./mhCustomMaterialCallbackCmd.cpp) adds the *mhRegisterCustomMaterialCallback* command, which registers a callback replacing the GLSLShader materials using *myCustomShader.ogsfx* by the *FVP_CustomBufferShader* GLSLFX shader, reading that stream.

Python usage :

    cmds.mhRegisterCustomMaterialCallback()
    cmds.mhRegisterCustomMaterialCallback(unregister=True)

## Replacing a Maya material by a custom Hydra material

The interface to create custom Hydra materials is called *[MaterialCreationInterface](../../../flowViewport/API/fvpMaterialCreationInterface.h)*.
To get an instance of the *MaterialCreationInterface* class, please use :

    Fvp::MaterialCreationInterface& materialCreationInterface = Fvp::MaterialCreationInterface::Get();

It lets you register a callback under a name. When MayaHydra translates a Maya material whose name matches, it calls your callback instead of doing its own conversion, and the Hydra material network you return is used as is.

At the time of this writing, only Maya surface shaders of type **GLSLShader** are supported. The name used to find the callback is the file name of the GLSLShader *shader* attribute, without its directory and extension : *"C:/shaders/myCustomShader.ogsfx"* gives *"myCustomShader"*. So all the GLSLShader nodes pointing at that file, wherever it is located, use your callback.

A typical use is a Maya OGSFX shader that reads a **custom vertex buffer**, created by a Maya *MPxVertexBufferGenerator*, which should also be drawn by Hydra Storm. MayaHydra passes such a buffer to Hydra as a primvar named after its custom semantic, so a GLSLFX shader can read it.

### How it works

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    ogsfx["GLSLShader node<br/>shader = myCustomShader.ogsfx"]
    gen["MPxVertexBufferGenerator<br/>for semantic myCustomStreamB"]
    vb["VP2 custom vertex buffer<br/>myCustomStreamB"]
    cb["Your callback registered<br/>under myCustomShader"]
    net["Hydra material network<br/>using FVP_CustomBufferShader"]
    primvar["Hydra primvar<br/>myCustomStreamB"]
    glslfx["GLSLFX shader reads<br/>HdGet_myCustomStreamB()"]
    ogsfx -->|customSemantics annotation| gen
    gen --> vb
    vb -->|MayaHydra render item adapter| primvar
    ogsfx -->|MayaHydra material adapter| cb
    cb --> net
    net --> glslfx
    primvar --> glslfx
```

### Step by step

This example and the [shadersDiscoveryPlugin](../../../flowViewport/usdPlugins/shadersDiscoveryPlugin) example implement every step below, the file names given in each step are where to look.

**1. Write the Maya OGSFX shader and declare the custom semantic.**
In the technique annotation, map a vertex input semantic to your custom semantic name, see [myCustomShader.ogsfx](../../../flowViewport/usdPlugins/shadersDiscoveryPlugin/shaders/myCustomShader.ogsfx) :

    technique Main
    <
        string customSemantics = "TEXCOORD0=myCustomStreamB";
    >

The custom semantic name becomes the Hydra primvar name, so it must be a valid primvar name, and it must **not** start with *"uv"* or *"mayauv"* : these are treated as UV sets and published as the *st* primvar.

**2. Register a vertex buffer generator for that semantic.**
VP2 only creates the custom vertex buffer if an *MPxVertexBufferGenerator* is registered for the semantic, see [mhCustomVertexBufferGenerator.cpp](./mhCustomVertexBufferGenerator.cpp) :

    MHWRender::MDrawRegistry::registerVertexBufferGenerator("myCustomStreamB", MyGenerator::creator);

Fill the stream with **float** values of **2, 3 or 4 components**, one per vertex of the target indexing, like a UV set. Other data types are not translated to Hydra. Deregister the generator when your plugin unloads.

**3. Write the GLSLFX shader for Hydra Storm.**
Read the primvar with *HdGet_\<semantic name\>()*, and guard it with *HD_HAS_\<semantic name\>* so the shader still compiles on geometry that does not provide it, see [customBufferShader.glslfx](../../../flowViewport/usdPlugins/shadersDiscoveryPlugin/shaders/customBufferShader.glslfx) :

    vec4 surfaceShader(vec4 Peye, vec3 Neye, vec4 color, vec4 patchCoord)
    {
    #ifdef HD_HAS_myCustomStreamB
        vec4 customStream = HdGet_myCustomStreamB();
    #else
        vec4 customStream = vec4(0);
    #endif
        return vec4(customStream.xyz * -0.5 + vec3(0.5), 1.0);
    }

**4. Register the GLSLFX shader in USD and list the primvars it reads.**
Add a *Shader* definition to a shaders discovery plugin, see [shaderDefs.usda](../../../flowViewport/usdPlugins/shadersDiscoveryPlugin/shaders/shaderDefs.usda) and the [shadersDiscoveryPlugin README](../../../flowViewport/usdPlugins/shadersDiscoveryPlugin/README) on how USD finds it :

    def Shader "FVP_CustomBufferShader" (
        sdrMetadata = {
            string primvars = "myCustomStreamB"
        }
    )
    {
        uniform token info:implementationSource = "sourceAsset"
        uniform asset info:glslfx:sourceAsset = @./customBufferShader.glslfx@
        token outputs:displacement
        token outputs:surface
    }

The *primvars* metadata is required : Hydra Storm only gives a material the primvars it asks for, so without it *HD_HAS_myCustomStreamB* is never defined. To read several primvars, separate their names with *|*.

**5. Write the material creation callback.**
It receives the Hydra path of the material prim and returns an *HdMaterialNetworkMap* in a *VtValue*, see [mhCustomMaterialCallbackCmd.cpp](./mhCustomMaterialCallbackCmd.cpp) :

    VtValue CreateMyMaterial(const SdfPath& materialId)
    {
        HdMaterialNetworkMap networkMap;
        HdMaterialNetwork&   network = networkMap.map[HdMaterialTerminalTokens->surface];

        HdMaterialNode terminal;
        terminal.identifier = TfToken("FVP_CustomBufferShader");
        terminal.path = materialId.AppendChild(terminal.identifier);

        networkMap.terminals.push_back(terminal.path);
        network.nodes.emplace_back(std::move(terminal));
        return VtValue(networkMap);
    }

- *identifier* is the name of the *Shader* definition from step 4.
- Put the node paths under *materialId*, so the network stays inside the material prim.
- The GLSLShader parameters are **not** translated. If your GLSLFX shader has inputs, set them yourself in *terminal.parameters*.
- Return an empty *VtValue* to let MayaHydra use its default conversion for that material.

**6. Register the callback, and unregister it before your plugin unloads.**
Use the shader file name of step 1, without directory and extension :

    Fvp::MaterialCreationInterface::Get().RegisterMaterialCreationCallback("myCustomShader", CreateMyMaterial);
    ...
    Fvp::MaterialCreationInterface::Get().UnregisterMaterialCreationCallback("myCustomShader");

Registering again under the same name replaces the previous callback. The callback is code from your plugin, so **always unregister it in your plugin's *uninitializePlugin***, otherwise MayaHydra would call it after your plugin is unloaded.
Register it before the Hydra viewport translates your materials. Materials which were already translated are not converted again when you register the callback.

**7. Use it in Maya.**
Assign a GLSLShader to a mesh and set its *shader* attribute to your OGSFX file, then switch the viewport to Hydra Storm. This example plugin does it with the *mhRegisterCustomMaterialCallback* command, see the [testCustomMaterial.py](../../../../test/lib/mayaUsd/render/mayaToHydra/testCustomMaterial.py) test :

    cmds.loadPlugin("glslShader")
    cmds.loadPlugin("mayaHydraCustomShadersNode")
    cmds.mhRegisterCustomMaterialCallback()

    sphere = cmds.polySphere()[0]
    shader = cmds.shadingNode("GLSLShader", asShader=True)
    sg = cmds.sets(renderable=True, noSurfaceShader=True, empty=True, name=shader + "SG")
    cmds.connectAttr(shader + ".outColor", sg + ".surfaceShader")
    cmds.sets(sphere, edit=True, forceElement=sg)
    cmds.setAttr(shader + ".shader", "<path to>/myCustomShader.ogsfx", type="string")

### Limitations
- Only GLSLShader surface shaders call the material creation callbacks.
- Custom vertex buffers are translated on the default render item path only. When the render item override path is used, set the environment variable *MAYAHYDRA_RENDER_ITEM_OVERRIDE=0* before starting Maya to get them.
- Only float custom vertex buffers of 2 to 4 components are translated, on meshes.

### Troubleshooting
- **The mesh is drawn with the default Maya conversion** : check that the callback name is the shader file name without directory and extension, and that it was registered before the material was translated.
- **The mesh is drawn with your shader, but *HD_HAS_\<semantic name\>* is not defined** : check the *primvars* metadata of step 4, that a vertex buffer generator is registered for the semantic (step 2), and that the semantic name does not start with *"uv"* or *"mayauv"*. Setting the environment variable *TF_DEBUG=MAYAHYDRALIB_ADAPTER_GPU_BUFFER_SHARING* prints which vertex streams MayaHydra shares with Hydra on the GPU, custom ones included.
- **A warning says a vertex buffer generator is already registered for the custom semantic** : another plugin, such as the Maya devkit *vertexBufferGenerator* plugin, registered a generator for the same semantic first, and that one fills the buffer.
